Docker Images, Containers, and Docker Hub
Cloud Computing Lab Exam — Command Reference

Based on the supplied question bank: Experiment 8 (Q49–Q53) and Experiment 9 (Q54–Q64). Commands assume Ubuntu/Linux with Docker installed. Replace example names/ports only when needed.

A. Common setup — install and verify Docker

# Install Docker on Ubuntu
sudo apt update
sudo apt install docker.io -y

# Start Docker and enable it at boot
sudo systemctl enable --now docker

# Verify installation
docker --version
sudo docker run hello-world

If your lab account does not have permission to run Docker without sudo, keep using sudo before Docker commands. Do not change permissions on a shared lab machine unless your instructor authorizes it.

EXPERIMENT 8 — Creating and executing Docker containers/images

Q49 (Original Q4) — Run a Hello World container

sudo docker run hello-world

Expected: Docker downloads the image if needed, runs it, and prints a successful installation message.

Q50 (Original Q20) — Create, start, stop, restart and inspect status

# Create a container
sudo docker create --name labbox ubuntu:latest

# Start it
sudo docker start labbox

# Show container status
sudo docker ps -a

# Stop it
sudo docker stop labbox

# Restart it
sudo docker start labbox

# Show status again
sudo docker ps -a

# Remove it when finished
sudo docker rm -f labbox

A newly created Ubuntu container exits when its main process ends. Use Q61 for an interactive shell.

Q51 (Original Q28) — Build an image using a Dockerfile

mkdir -p ~/docker-lab
cd ~/docker-lab

cat > Dockerfile <<'EOF'
FROM ubuntu:latest
CMD ["echo", "Hello from my custom Docker image!"]
EOF

sudo docker build -t myhello:1.0 .
sudo docker images
sudo docker run --name myhello-container myhello:1.0

Q52 (Original Q40) — List, inspect and remove images/containers

# List running containers
sudo docker ps

# List all containers, including stopped ones
sudo docker ps -a

# List downloaded images
sudo docker images

# Inspect a container
sudo docker inspect myhello-container

# View container logs
sudo docker logs myhello-container

# Remove the container
sudo docker rm myhello-container

# Remove the image (only after its container is removed)
sudo docker rmi myhello:1.0

Q53 (Original Q52) — Build and run a simple web app image

mkdir -p ~/web-lab
cd ~/web-lab

echo '<h1>Hello from Docker Web App!</h1>' > index.html

cat > Dockerfile <<'EOF'
FROM nginx:alpine
COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
EOF

sudo docker build -t myweb:1.0 .
sudo docker run -d --name myweb-container -p 8080:80 myweb:1.0

# Test in browser at http://localhost:8080
# If on a remote VM, use its reachable IP address and port 8080.

sudo docker logs myweb-container
sudo docker stop myweb-container
sudo docker rm myweb-container

EXPERIMENT 9 — Running and managing containers from Docker Hub

Q54 (Original Q8) — Pull Linux image and run commands

sudo docker pull ubuntu:latest
sudo docker run --name ubuntu-lab -it ubuntu:latest
# Inside the container, run:
ls
pwd
mkdir demo
cd demo
touch sample.txt
ls -l
exit

# After exiting, check container
sudo docker ps -a

